@@s007 Reducing to a flow: recipe and gadget
@@s008 The flow puts capacities on the arcs, but the constraints usually concern the nodes: a node used at most once, or a vertex to remove. The split gadget turns a node constraint into an arc constraint.
@@s009 Splitting the node
@@s010 Replace the node <code>v</code> with two nodes <code>v_in</code> and <code>v_out</code>, joined by <code>v_in → v_out</code> with capacity <code>c(v)</code>. The arcs that entered <code>v</code> now enter <code>v_in</code>; those that left it leave from <code>v_out</code>.
@@s011 Every flow that passes through <code>v</code> must cross that arc: so it cannot exceed <code>c(v)</code>. The cost doubles the nodes and adds <code>n</code> arcs: same order of magnitude.
@@s012 The node v becomes two nodes with an arc of capacity 1
@@s013 Where it is used
@@s014 Paths <b>node-disjoint</b> (each node used by only one path), <b>minimum number of nodes</b> to remove to separate s from t (node cut), and "special" nodes that cannot be removed (infinite capacity).
@@s015 The recipe: reduce to a flow
@@s016 For each problem you want to solve with the flow, answer five questions in order. It is the same recipe for matching, pairs and disjoint paths:
@@s017 <b>The nodes</b>: which and how many, as a function of the parameters of the problem. Usually: <code>s</code>, <code>t</code>, one node for each object of type A, one for each object of type B.
@@s018 <b>The arcs</b>: from where to where, oriented, and how many in total.
@@s019 <b>The capacities</b>: each with its meaning. Capacity 1 means "at most once". Capacity <code>∞</code> means "no limit, but the arc cannot be cut".
@@s020 <b>The meaning of the flow</b>: what a maximum flow means in the original problem, and what it is worth. It must be proved in both directions: from a solution a flow arises, and from an integer flow a solution arises.
@@s021 <b>The time</b>: which flow algorithm and with which parameters. Ford-Fulkerson costs O(m·C), where <code>m</code> are the arcs and <code>C</code> the maximum value of the flow.
@@s022 Order of work: first draw the example graph, precisely on nodes, arcs and how many they are. Then write the general rule. Whoever skips the drawing almost always gets a capacity wrong.
@@s023 At the exam
@@s024 At the exam
@@s025 Constraint "a node at most once" → split of the node with capacity 1. Phrase: <i>"Split each node v into v_in and v_out joined by an arc of capacity 1; arcs into v enter v_in, arcs out of v leave v_out."</i>
